14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"December 1860–August 1862, with consequences through January 1, 1863","place":"Washington, D.C.; the loyal slave states of Delaware, Maryland, Kentucky, and Missouri; Fort Monroe and Hampton Roads, Virginia; and the wider United States","result":"The United States entered the war with an official policy of restoring the Union without attacking slavery where it existed. Enslaved people seeking protection behind United States lines, abolitionist and Radical Republican pressure, congressional action, military reverses, and the demonstrated value of enslaved labor to the Confederacy steadily weakened that limited-war policy. Benjamin Butler’s May 1861 refusal to return Frank Baker, Shepard Mallory, and James Townsend at Fort Monroe became an early operational break with conciliation and introduced the “contraband” policy developed in the next episode","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"Why United States policy toward slavery began as limited war","note":"The episode sets out the constitutional, military, electoral, and border-state calculations behind conciliation, then traces the pressure for emancipation to Fort Monroe in May 1861."},{"label":"Central distinction","value":"The cause of secession was not the same as the United States government’s initial war aim","note":"Slaveholding states seceded to protect slavery. Lincoln and Congress initially defined the federal war aim as suppressing rebellion and restoring the Union, not abolishing slavery in the states."},{"label":"Frederick Douglass’s position","value":"War should be used to destroy slavery and the slaveholders’ power","note":"Douglass argued that enslaved labor sustained the Confederate economy and military effort and that liberation would convert a Confederate resource into a United States advantage."},{"label":"Abolitionist forecast","value":"Secession and war would force the slavery question into federal policy","note":"The episode opens with Douglass in December 1860 and Joshua Giddings after Fort Sumter treating the crisis as the beginning of slavery’s destruction."},{"label":"Conservative opposition","value":"Federal authority could not constitutionally abolish slavery inside a state","note":"Kentucky Representative Aaron Harding represents the view that the war should leave slavery untouched. Lincoln and most of the contemporary political establishment also recognized state control over slavery in peacetime."},{"label":"Lincoln to Alexander H. Stephens","value":"December 22, 1860","note":"Lincoln privately assured the Georgia Unionist that Southern people had no reason to fear that his administration would directly or indirectly interfere with slavery in their states."},{"label":"First Inaugural Address","value":"March 4, 1861","note":"Lincoln publicly repeated that he had no lawful right or intention to interfere with slavery where it already existed."},{"label":"Initial military policy","value":"Suppress rebellion while avoiding unnecessary interference with Southern property","note":"The administration hoped restraint would preserve a broad Northern coalition, retain loyal slave states, and encourage Southern Unionists to end secession."},{"label":"Lincoln’s personal position","value":"Antislavery, but not an abolitionist in 1861","note":"Lincoln considered slavery unjust and opposed its expansion. He also held racist views, supported voluntary colonization, and initially favored gradual, compensated emancipation through state action."},{"label":"Colonization","value":"Voluntary emigration of free Black people outside the United States","note":"Lincoln’s support for colonization reflected his doubt that Black and white Americans could live together as equals. The policy remained part of his emancipation proposals into 1862."},{"label":"Federalism constraint","value":"State governments controlled slavery within their borders in peacetime","note":"A nationwide federal abolition law was not considered constitutionally available in 1861. The later proclamation rested instead on the president’s commander-in-chief power during an armed rebellion and applied only to designated rebellious territory."},{"label":"Political coalition constraint","value":"Lincoln won 54 percent of the free-state popular vote in 1860","note":"A large minority of Northern voters had supported Stephen A. Douglas, John C. Breckinridge, or John Bell. Lincoln needed many of those voters to sustain a long war."},{"label":"Loyal slave states","value":"Delaware, Maryland, Kentucky, and Missouri","note":"Their location, people, transportation routes, and military resources made their continued loyalty essential. Washington itself bordered slaveholding Maryland and Virginia."},{"label":"“Lose Kentucky” letter","value":"Lincoln to Orville H. Browning, September 22, 1861","note":"Defending his modification of John C. Frémont’s Missouri emancipation order, Lincoln wrote that losing Kentucky was nearly the same as losing the entire contest and could also cost Missouri, Maryland, and Washington."},{"label":"Policy of conciliation","value":"Restore federal authority without making reunification harder","note":"It relied on an exaggerated expectation that a suppressed Southern Unionist majority would reassert itself if the federal government avoided attacking slavery and other local institutions."},{"label":"Crittenden Resolution","value":"House adopted July 22, 1861","note":"The House declared that the war was not waged for conquest, subjugation, or interference with the established institutions of the rebelling states. The operative section passed 117–2."},{"label":"Johnson Resolution","value":"Senate adopted July 25, 1861","note":"Andrew Johnson of Tennessee introduced the Senate’s substantially identical declaration of limited war aims; it passed 30–5."},{"label":"Transcript vote correction","value":"117–2 in the House and 30–5 in the Senate","note":"The transcript says 119–2 and 35–5. The House Journal and Congressional Globe record 117–2 for the operative House section and 30–5 for the Senate resolution."},{"label":"Lincoln’s first annual message","value":"December 3, 1861","note":"Lincoln said the Union must be preserved but warned against haste in deciding that radical and extreme measures were indispensable. He still sought compensated emancipation and colonization rather than immediate general emancipation."},{"label":"Military-necessity argument","value":"Attack slavery because enslaved labor sustained the Confederate war effort","note":"Abolitionists and congressional Radicals emphasized that liberation would remove labor from Confederate fortifications, farms, and logistics while adding workers and eventually soldiers to the United States cause."},{"label":"Enslaved people as political actors","value":"They forced policy decisions by escaping to United States lines","note":"Self-emancipation confronted commanders with immediate choices about return, protection, work, and legal status. Federal policy did not change through elite debate alone."},{"label":"Fort Monroe freedom seekers","value":"Frank Baker, Shepard Mallory, and James Townsend","note":"The three enslaved men escaped from work on Confederate defenses at Sewell’s Point during the night of May 23–24, 1861, and sought protection at Union-held Fort Monroe."},{"label":"Butler’s decision","value":"Refuse the enslaver’s demand for their return","note":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin F. Butler reasoned that Virginia claimed to be outside United States law while using the men’s coerced labor for war. He treated that labor as enemy property subject to seizure and withheld the men from their enslaver."},{"label":"Meaning of “contraband”","value":"A wartime property classification, not a declaration of full legal freedom","note":"The policy blocked return and offered practical protection, but it preserved the law’s degrading treatment of people as property and left their exact legal status uncertain."},{"label":"Immediate consequence at Fort Monroe","value":"Hundreds of additional freedom seekers reached the fort","note":"The National Park Service reports that roughly 900 men, women, and children had arrived within a little more than a month of Butler’s decision."},{"label":"Congressional consequence","value":"First Confiscation Act, August 6, 1861","note":"Congress provided that an enslaver forfeited claims to the labor of people employed in aid of the rebellion. The act did not abolish slavery generally and left many questions unresolved."},{"label":"Public opinion changed unevenly","value":"Military reverses increased support for attacking slavery","note":"The episode cites antislavery petitions, larger abolitionist audiences, press criticism, and John Sherman’s August 1862 letter as evidence that military necessity moved some moderates toward emancipation."},{"label":"John Sherman’s August 1862 position","value":"Use Black Americans as allies and accept broad emancipation","note":"The Ohio senator told his brother William T. Sherman that Northern opinion had changed and that the United States should enlist Black assistance in every way that advanced the national cause."},{"label":"What the final proclamation did","value":"Declared enslaved people in designated rebellious areas free and authorized Black enlistment","note":"It did not abolish slavery nationwide, did not apply to loyal slave states, and depended on United States military power for enforcement. Nearly 200,000 Black soldiers and sailors served by the war’s end."},{"label":"Watch Night, December 31, 1862","value":"Black communities gathered to await the proclamation’s effective date","note":"The episode describes about 600 people at a Washington contraband camp singing, recounting experiences of slavery, and praying before midnight. Watch Night observances occurred across free and enslaved Black communities."},{"label":"No battle losses in this episode","value":"The narrative concerns policy, political authority, and self-emancipation","note":"No military leader is killed or mortally wounded within the events narrated. The losses section is therefore intentionally empty."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Fall of the House of Dixie” by Bruce Levine","note":"The full subtitle is “The Civil War and the Social Revolution That Transformed the South.”"},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"George E. Stephens, Orville H. Browning, Shepard Mallory, and First Manassas","note":"The transcript renders or omits several names and produces “Lincoln fighted.” The documented wording is that Lincoln wrote to Browning; standard historical names and forms are used here."}],"maps":[{"title":"Fort Monroe and the first three freedom seekers","caption":"During the night of May 23–24, 1861, Frank Baker, Shepard Mallory, and James Townsend escaped from coerced work on Confederate gun positions at Sewell’s Point and crossed Hampton Roads to Union-held Fort Monroe. Butler interviewed them on May 24 and refused their enslaver’s demand for return. The route and shorelines are generalized and the map is not to scale.","source":"Fort Monroe National Monument, National Park Service; Library of Congress; and episode transcript. See sources below.","image":"/images/ep201-fort-monroe-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Hampton Roads showing the route of Frank Baker, Shepard Mallory, and James Townsend from Sewell’s Point to Fort Monroe."},{"title":"From limited war to emancipation policy","caption":"The federal position changed in stages. Lincoln began with constitutional assurances and conciliation; self-emancipation and Butler’s Fort Monroe decision created operational pressure; Congress, abolitionists, military reverses, and changing public opinion expanded the policy debate; Lincoln ultimately used commander-in-chief authority for the preliminary and final proclamations.","source":"Abraham Lincoln Papers, National Archives, U.S. congressional documents, National Park Service, and episode transcript. See sources below.","image":"/images/ep201-emancipation-policy-timeline.svg","alt":"Timeline of United States policy from Lincoln’s December 1860 assurance through the final Emancipation Proclamation on January 1, 1863."},{"title":"Frederick Douglass","caption":"Douglass argued from the opening of the crisis that destroying slavery was both a moral obligation and a military necessity. His analysis centers the enslaved labor that sustained the Confederacy and the power that self-emancipation could add to the United States war effort.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of abolitionist Frederick Douglass."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:11","audioTime":71,"title":"Douglass reads secession as an opening against slavery","detail":"The episode begins in December 1860 with Frederick Douglass arguing that disunion and federal military action could force the nation to confront slavery."},{"time":"2:14","audioTime":134,"title":"Joshua Giddings predicts slavery’s destruction","detail":"After Fort Sumter, the Ohio antislavery politician tells Charles Sumner that the first shot of the war sounded slavery’s death knell."},{"time":"2:40","audioTime":160,"title":"Aaron Harding states the conservative position","detail":"The Kentucky representative denies federal power over slavery inside a state and insists that restoring the Union should be separated from emancipation."},{"time":"3:01","audioTime":181,"title":"Military events begin to overtake limited policy","detail":"The hosts frame the government’s 1861–62 emancipation policies as responses to an intensifying and unexpectedly difficult war."},{"time":"3:26","audioTime":206,"title":"The new emancipation story arc is defined","detail":"The series will trace how federal ideas and policy changed, with Lincoln’s proclamation as the central document rather than the only cause of emancipation."},{"time":"4:13","audioTime":253,"title":"Lincoln’s assessment of the proclamation is introduced","detail":"The episode presents the proclamation as the central act of Lincoln’s administration and a decisive nineteenth-century event."},{"time":"4:23","audioTime":263,"title":"The argument over meaning and intent begins","detail":"The hosts distinguish the proclamation’s practical and legal operation from later debates about Lincoln’s motives."},{"time":"4:57","audioTime":297,"title":"The proclamation’s geographic limits are stated","detail":"Critics note that it applied in designated rebellious territory rather than loyal slave states or all places already controlled by federal forces."},{"time":"5:13","audioTime":313,"title":"Richard Hofstadter’s 1948 criticism is presented","detail":"The historian’s influential attack on the proclamation’s legal language and supposed lack of practical purpose becomes the episode’s principal interpretation to rebut."},{"time":"6:06","audioTime":366,"title":"Legal phrasing is treated as purposeful","detail":"The episode argues that the document’s restrained form reflected the need to ground emancipation in a defensible exercise of wartime executive power."},{"time":"6:36","audioTime":396,"title":"Military advance becomes enforcement","detail":"After January 1, 1863, each federal advance into designated rebellious territory could give practical effect to the freedom the proclamation recognized."},{"time":"6:55","audioTime":415,"title":"Black enlistment becomes federal policy","detail":"The final proclamation authorizes the armed service to receive Black men, allowing people escaping slavery to serve directly in the war against the Confederacy."},{"time":"7:41","audioTime":461,"title":"Formerly enslaved people interpret the event","detail":"The episode shifts attention from later academic criticism to Black people who understood emancipation as a direct change in their lives and prospects."},{"time":"8:22","audioTime":502,"title":"A Washington gathering waits for midnight","detail":"About 600 Black men, women, and children sing, recount experiences of slavery, and kneel in prayer shortly before the proclamation’s effective date."},{"time":"9:22","audioTime":562,"title":"George E. Stephens searches for a revolutionary leader","detail":"The Black newspaper correspondent compares the Civil War with England’s seventeenth-century revolution and writes that the American crisis has no Cromwell."},{"time":"10:19","audioTime":619,"title":"Lincoln and most Republicans reject revolution","detail":"They oppose slavery’s expansion and expect its eventual extinction but prefer gradual political change to immediate wartime social transformation."},{"time":"11:18","audioTime":678,"title":"The initial expectation is a brief, limited war","detail":"Republicans plan to suppress rebellion while avoiding actions they believe would alienate white Southerners and impede reunion."},{"time":"11:48","audioTime":708,"title":"Conciliation is named as federal policy","detail":"Government and military policy changes only incrementally as assumptions about Confederate weakness and Southern Unionism prove false."},{"time":"12:05","audioTime":725,"title":"Attacking slavery becomes a means to victory","detail":"The decisive policy turn comes when slavery is understood as essential to Confederate strength and emancipation as necessary to United States victory."},{"time":"12:28","audioTime":748,"title":"Self-emancipation changes the calculation","detail":"Enslaved people demonstrate through their own actions that leaving plantations and reaching federal lines can weaken the Confederacy and strengthen the United States."},{"time":"13:49","audioTime":829,"title":"Lincoln’s early assurances are reviewed","detail":"From the secession crisis into the war, Lincoln repeatedly says that he will not interfere with slavery where it already exists."},{"time":"14:01","audioTime":841,"title":"Lincoln writes Alexander H. Stephens","detail":"On December 22, 1860, the president-elect assures the Georgia Unionist that his administration poses no direct or indirect threat to slavery in the states."},{"time":"14:38","audioTime":878,"title":"Secession continues despite the assurances","detail":"The Lower South leaves the Union, showing that conciliatory statements do not reverse the political movement for a slaveholding Confederacy."},{"time":"14:52","audioTime":892,"title":"The limited policy continues after Fort Sumter","detail":"Lincoln calls troops to suppress rebellion while cautioning against unnecessary destruction of or interference with Southern property."},{"time":"15:13","audioTime":913,"title":"Public restraint is separated from personal conviction","detail":"The hosts argue that Lincoln’s limited policy did not erase his long-held belief that slavery was unjust."},{"time":"15:21","audioTime":921,"title":"Lincoln’s racial views and political limits are stated","detail":"In 1861 he is not an abolitionist, does not advocate racial equality, and doubts peaceful interracial citizenship."},{"time":"15:35","audioTime":935,"title":"Voluntary colonization is defined","detail":"Lincoln supports federal aid for free Black people who consent to emigrate outside the United States, a policy tied to his racial assumptions."},{"time":"15:44","audioTime":944,"title":"Lincoln’s antislavery conviction is quoted","detail":"The episode uses his later statement that slavery is wrong and that he had long thought so to distinguish belief from the means he initially considered lawful and practical."},{"time":"16:11","audioTime":971,"title":"Republican free-labor criticism is explained","detail":"Republicans also oppose slavery because they believe it blocks economic opportunity and corrodes republican government."},{"time":"16:28","audioTime":988,"title":"War is not initially the instrument of abolition","detail":"Lincoln plans to restore the Union first and resume the political containment of slavery afterward."},{"time":"17:01","audioTime":1021,"title":"The first annual message defends limited war","detail":"On December 3, 1861, Lincoln warns against allowing the conflict to become a violent revolutionary struggle before radical measures are indispensable."},{"time":"17:34","audioTime":1054,"title":"Two constraints organize Lincoln’s policy","detail":"The episode identifies constitutional federalism and the practical requirements of military victory as separate but related concerns."},{"time":"17:48","audioTime":1068,"title":"State authority over slavery is described","detail":"The dominant peacetime constitutional view places authority over slavery inside a state with that state, not with the federal government."},{"time":"18:19","audioTime":1099,"title":"Lincoln overestimates Southern Unionism","detail":"He assumes many residents of the seceded states were coerced or misled and might restore loyal government if federal policy remains restrained."},{"time":"19:02","audioTime":1142,"title":"Northern unity becomes another constraint","detail":"Lincoln believes a limited war aim is necessary to retain voters who support the Union but do not support immediate abolition."},{"time":"19:17","audioTime":1157,"title":"The 1860 free-state vote is parsed","detail":"Lincoln won a majority in the free states, but nearly half of their voters chose one of three more conservative opponents."},{"time":"19:57","audioTime":1197,"title":"The loyal slave states become decisive","detail":"Delaware, Maryland, Kentucky, and Missouri have little Republican support, yet their continued loyalty is essential to the United States war effort."},{"time":"20:10","audioTime":1210,"title":"Lincoln explains Kentucky’s importance","detail":"In a September 1861 letter to Orville H. Browning, he argues that losing Kentucky could also cost Missouri, Maryland, and the capital."},{"time":"20:35","audioTime":1235,"title":"Border-state politicians gain policy leverage","detail":"Their states’ strategic value gives Unionists from slaveholding states influence beyond their numbers in the Republican coalition."},{"time":"20:52","audioTime":1252,"title":"Congress declares limited war aims","detail":"Immediately after First Manassas, the House says the war seeks constitutional supremacy and restored Union, not conquest or interference with state institutions."},{"time":"21:26","audioTime":1286,"title":"Crittenden and Johnson sponsor the resolutions","detail":"Kentucky Representative John J. Crittenden and Tennessee Senator Andrew Johnson are slave-state Unionists who reject secession without embracing abolition."},{"time":"21:48","audioTime":1308,"title":"Republicans largely support the declaration","detail":"The House and Senate approve the resolutions overwhelmingly, documenting how narrow the official federal war aim remained in July 1861."},{"time":"22:15","audioTime":1335,"title":"Abolitionists reject conciliation","detail":"Black and white abolitionists argue that leaving slavery untouched is both morally wrong and incapable of dividing or placating Confederate slaveholders."},{"time":"22:30","audioTime":1350,"title":"Douglass identifies slaveholding solidarity","detail":"He warns that Lincoln has mistaken the depth of slaveholders’ commitment to the Confederacy and cannot count on them to restore the Union."},{"time":"22:51","audioTime":1371,"title":"The abolitionist objective is to break slaveholders’ power","detail":"Douglass argues that federal safety requires direct action against the labor system that supplies Confederate economic and military strength."},{"time":"23:16","audioTime":1396,"title":"Enslaved labor is called the key to the rebellion","detail":"The episode explains how coerced labor builds defenses, supports armies, and keeps plantations producing while white men serve in Confederate ranks."},{"time":"23:58","audioTime":1438,"title":"Emancipation is framed as military necessity","detail":"The Anti-Slavery Standard’s formulation—that victory without emancipation is militarily impossible—summarizes the abolitionist strategic argument."},{"time":"24:10","audioTime":1450,"title":"Douglass expects events to force a policy change","detail":"He predicts that the government may delay recognition of slavery’s military value but cannot avoid it indefinitely."},{"time":"24:44","audioTime":1484,"title":"Radical Republicans carry the argument into Congress","detail":"The episode distinguishes abolitionists outside government from the Republican Party’s Radical wing, which can convert antislavery strategy into legislation."},{"time":"25:07","audioTime":1507,"title":"Owen Lovejoy represents moral radicalism","detail":"The Illinois representative, brother of murdered abolitionist editor Elijah Lovejoy, condemns slaveholding as a concentration of crimes."},{"time":"25:28","audioTime":1528,"title":"Thaddeus Stevens leads in the House","detail":"Stevens opposes the promise not to interfere with slavery and expects the demands of war to produce federal action against it."},{"time":"26:01","audioTime":1561,"title":"The congressional Radical network is named","detail":"Stevens, Lovejoy, Zachariah Chandler, Benjamin Wade, Joshua Giddings, George W. Julian, and Charles Sumner press variants of the military-necessity case."},{"time":"26:55","audioTime":1615,"title":"Sumner advises a military argument","detail":"He counsels allies to present emancipation as necessary to victory, a constitutional and political basis with broader reach than philanthropy alone."},{"time":"27:08","audioTime":1628,"title":"Battlefield failure strengthens the case","detail":"Lack of decisive success makes measures that reduce Confederate resources and add to federal strength more urgent."},{"time":"27:22","audioTime":1642,"title":"Federal advances expose slavery’s military role","detail":"Soldiers moving into slaveholding territory see coerced labor supporting Confederate farms, transport, camps, and fortifications."},{"time":"27:34","audioTime":1654,"title":"Antislavery politics gains public support","detail":"The costs of war and repeated Confederate victories make previously marginalized abolitionist arguments more acceptable in the North."},{"time":"27:44","audioTime":1664,"title":"Douglass’s reception in Syracuse changes","detail":"A city where an attempt to hear him had produced a riot early in 1861 welcomes him by the end of the year."},{"time":"28:22","audioTime":1702,"title":"Military setbacks produce petitions for a harder policy","detail":"Citizens petition Congress and write newspapers demanding that the administration use emancipation against the rebellion."},{"time":"28:48","audioTime":1728,"title":"John Sherman describes changing Northern opinion","detail":"After the Peninsula Campaign, the moderate Ohio senator tells his brother that supporters of Union increasingly favor using Black Americans as allies."},{"time":"29:40","audioTime":1780,"title":"Sherman accepts broad emancipation","detail":"The episode uses his willingness to meet the issue of universal emancipation as evidence that the argument has moved beyond the Radical minority."},{"time":"29:58","audioTime":1798,"title":"Benjamin Butler enters the narrative","detail":"The Massachusetts Democrat and political general commands Fort Monroe, where enslaved people force an immediate wartime decision."},{"time":"30:11","audioTime":1811,"title":"Three men reach Fort Monroe","detail":"Frank Baker, Shepard Mallory, and James Townsend escape coerced labor on Confederate defenses and seek protection behind federal lines in May 1861."},{"time":"30:24","audioTime":1824,"title":"The episode stops at the contraband decision","detail":"Butler’s refusal to return the three men becomes the starting point for the next installment’s account of federal confiscation and emancipation policy."},{"time":"30:48","audioTime":1848,"title":"The book recommendation begins","detail":"The hosts recommend Bruce Levine’s “The Fall of the House of Dixie,” a study of the war’s destruction of slavery and transformation of Southern society."}],"command":[{"side":"United States executive and military authority","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln","command":"President and constitutional commander in chief","subordinates":"Set executive war policy, directed cabinet departments and military commanders, protected the loyal slave-state coalition, and later invoked commander-in-chief power for emancipation in rebellious territory"},{"commander":"Secretary of War Simon Cameron","command":"Civilian head of the War Department through January 14, 1862","subordinates":"Supervised the Army under presidential authority during the Fort Monroe decision and the first phase of wartime slavery policy"},{"commander":"Secretary of War Edwin M. Stanton","command":"Civilian head of the War Department from January 15, 1862","subordinates":"Administered the Army as military reverses, congressional measures, Black labor, and emancipation became central federal war-policy questions"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin F. Butler","command":"Commander, Department of Virginia; headquarters at Fort Monroe","subordinates":"Interviewed Baker, Mallory, and Townsend on May 24, 1861, refused to return them, and reported his developing policy to Washington"}]},{"side":"Independent legislative and public pressure","color":"#8b4d3a","branches":[{"commander":"Thirty-seventh Congress","command":"Independent federal legislative authority","subordinates":"The House and Senate declared limited war aims in July 1861; Congress then enacted confiscation and other antislavery measures as policy changed"},{"commander":"Radical Republicans","command":"Congressional faction, not subordinate to the president","subordinates":"Thaddeus Stevens, Charles Sumner, Owen Lovejoy, Benjamin Wade, Zachariah Chandler, George W. Julian, and allies pressed emancipation as a military measure"},{"commander":"Abolitionists and the Black press","command":"Independent political movement","subordinates":"Frederick Douglass and other organizers, writers, speakers, and petitioners demanded that the United States destroy slavery and enlist Black power against the Confederacy"},{"commander":"Enslaved people seeking freedom","command":"Independent actors outside formal command structures","subordinates":"By leaving enslavers, withholding labor from the Confederacy, and reaching federal lines, they forced commanders and lawmakers to decide their legal and military status"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Frederick Douglass","role":"Black abolitionist, editor, and speaker who argued that destroying slavery was necessary to defeat the Confederacy","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645427/"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"President who began with limited war and conciliation, then adopted emancipation as a commander-in-chief war measure","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/"},{"name":"Frank Baker","role":"Enslaved man who escaped Confederate military labor at Sewell’s Point and sought protection at Fort Monroe","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep201-fort-monroe-map.svg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"name":"Shepard Mallory","role":"Enslaved man who escaped Confederate military labor at Sewell’s Point and sought protection at Fort Monroe","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep201-fort-monroe-map.svg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"name":"James Townsend","role":"Enslaved man who escaped Confederate military labor at Sewell’s Point and sought protection at Fort Monroe","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep201-fort-monroe-map.svg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"name":"Benjamin F. Butler","role":"Political general who refused to return the three Fort Monroe freedom seekers and treated their labor as enemy property","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/fort-monroe-and-the-contrabands-of-war.htm"},{"name":"George E. Stephens","role":"Black newspaper correspondent who looked for revolutionary leadership among United States forces in late 1861","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joshua R. Giddings","role":"Veteran Ohio antislavery politician who predicted after Fort Sumter that the war would destroy slavery","side":"United States","source":"https://history.house.gov/People/Listing/G/GIDDINGS,-Joshua-Reed-(G000167)/"},{"name":"Charles Sumner","role":"Massachusetts senator and Radical Republican who advanced emancipation as a military necessity","side":"United States","source":"https://www.senate.gov/artandhistory/history/common/generic/Featured_Bio_Sumner.htm"},{"name":"Thaddeus Stevens","role":"Pennsylvania representative and Radical leader who opposed the congressional pledge to leave slavery untouched","side":"United States","source":"https://history.house.gov/People/Listing/S/STEVENS,-Thaddeus-(S000887)/"},{"name":"Owen Lovejoy","role":"Illinois representative and abolitionist who demanded direct federal action against slavery","side":"United States","source":"https://history.house.gov/People/Listing/L/LOVEJOY,-Owen-(L000460)/"},{"name":"John J. Crittenden","role":"Kentucky Unionist representative who sponsored the House declaration of limited war aims","side":"United States","source":"https://history.house.gov/People/Listing/C/CRITTENDEN,-John-Jordan-(C000912)/"},{"name":"Andrew Johnson","role":"Tennessee Unionist senator who sponsored the Senate declaration of limited war aims","side":"United States","source":"https://bioguide.congress.gov/search/bio/J000116"},{"name":"John Sherman","role":"Ohio senator whose August 1862 letter documents a moderate Republican movement toward Black enlistment and broad emancipation","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/william-t-sherman-papers/about-this-collection/"},{"name":"Alexander H. Stephens","role":"Georgia Unionist in December 1860 who received Lincoln’s private assurance; later vice president of the Confederacy","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://teachingamericanhistory.org/document/letter-to-alexander-h-stephens/"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep201-fort-monroe-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the route from Sewell’s Point to Fort Monroe across Hampton Roads.","caption":"Baker, Mallory, and Townsend acted for their own freedom by escaping Confederate military labor and reaching federal lines. Their arrival made slavery an immediate command problem for Butler.","source":"National Park Service, Fort Monroe National Monument."},{"image":"/images/ep201-emancipation-policy-timeline.svg","alt":"Timeline from Lincoln’s December 1860 assurance to the final Emancipation Proclamation.","caption":"Federal policy changed through cumulative military, legal, political, and human pressure rather than through a single decision made at the war’s outset.","source":"Abraham Lincoln Papers, National Archives, U.S. congressional documents, National Park Service, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of Frederick Douglass on an oval studio mount.","caption":"Douglass insisted that the United States could not defeat slaveholders’ rebellion while protecting the labor system that made their war possible. This portrait dates to about 1862.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."},{"image":"/images/ep201-fort-monroe-contraband.jpg","alt":"An 1861 racist caricature showing Black freedom seekers running toward Fort Monroe while a white enslaver chases them.","caption":"This 1861 printed card uses racist caricature and slurs while recording a contemporary understanding of Fort Monroe as a destination that enslaved people could reach and from which enslavers could not easily reclaim them.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 201 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/07/23/civil-war-podcast-episode-201/"},{"label":"National Archives: Emancipation Proclamation and transcript","url":"https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/emancipation-proclamation"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Abraham Lincoln Papers","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln to Alexander H. Stephens, December 22, 1860","url":"https://teachingamericanhistory.org/document/letter-to-alexander-h-stephens/"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln’s First Inaugural Address manuscript","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/mal.0773800/?st=text"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln’s First Annual Message, December 3, 1861","url":"https://millercenter.org/the-presidency/presidential-speeches/december-3-1861-first-annual-message"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln to Orville H. Browning, September 22, 1861","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/mal.1192600/"},{"label":"Primary source: House Crittenden Resolution, July 1861","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/SERIALSET-01115_00_00-018-0017-0000/pdf/SERIALSET-01115_00_00-018-0017-0000.pdf"},{"label":"Primary source: Senate Johnson Resolution, July 1861","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/SERIALSET-01112_00_00-016-0007-0000/pdf/SERIALSET-01112_00_00-016-0007-0000.pdf"},{"label":"House Journal: July 22, 1861 vote on the Crittenden Resolution","url":"https://www.originalsources.com/Document.aspx?DocID=GJJIKSVR3GBER9D"},{"label":"National Park Service: Building 1 and the Fort Monroe freedom seekers","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Fort Monroe and the “Contrabands of War”","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/fort-monroe-and-the-contrabands-of-war.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Freedom’s Fortress","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/featured_stories_fomr.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: former slaves in wartime Washington","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/living-contraband-former-slaves-in-the-nation-s-capital-during-the-civil-war.htm"},{"label":"National Museum of African American History and Culture: Watch Night history","url":"https://nmaahc.si.edu/explore/stories/historical-legacy-watch-night"},{"label":"National Museum of African American History and Culture: “Waiting for the Hour”","url":"https://nmaahc.si.edu/object/nmaahc_2010.77.7"},{"label":"Library of Congress: William T. Sherman Papers and John Sherman’s August 24, 1862 letter","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/william-t-sherman-papers/about-this-collection/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Frederick Douglass portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645427/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: “Contraband, Fortress Monroe,” 1861","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2010652111/"},{"label":"Publisher page: “The Fall of the House of Dixie” by Bruce Levine","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/100479/the-fall-of-the-house-of-dixie-by-bruce-levine/"}]}}]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Frederick Douglass",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Frederick Douglass at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of abolitionist Frederick Douglass.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Frederick Douglass"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Douglass argued from the opening of the crisis that destroying slavery was both a moral obligation and a military necessity. His analysis centers the enslaved labor that sustained the Confederacy and the power that self-emancipation could add to the United States war effort."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States executive and military authority",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States executive and military authority"}],[["$","div","President and constitutional commander in chief-President Abraham Lincoln",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"President and constitutional commander in chief"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"President Abraham Lincoln"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Set executive war policy, directed cabinet departments and military commanders, protected the loyal slave-state coalition, and later invoked commander-in-chief power for emancipation in rebellious territory"}]]}],["$","div","Civilian head of the War Department through January 14, 1862-Secretary of War Simon Cameron",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Civilian head of the War Department through January 14, 1862"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Secretary of War Simon Cameron"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Supervised the Army under presidential authority during the Fort Monroe decision and the first phase of wartime slavery policy"}]]}],["$","div","Civilian head of the War Department from January 15, 1862-Secretary of War Edwin M. Stanton",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Civilian head of the War Department from January 15, 1862"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Secretary of War Edwin M. Stanton"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Administered the Army as military reverses, congressional measures, Black labor, and emancipation became central federal war-policy questions"}]]}],["$","div","Commander, Department of Virginia; headquarters at Fort Monroe-Maj. Gen. Benjamin F. Butler",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Commander, Department of Virginia; headquarters at Fort Monroe"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin F. Butler"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Interviewed Baker, Mallory, and Townsend on May 24, 1861, refused to return them, and reported his developing policy to Washington"}]]}]]]}],["$","section","Independent legislative and public pressure",{"className":"command-column #8b4d3a","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"Independent legislative and public pressure"}],[["$","div","Independent federal legislative authority-Thirty-seventh Congress",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Independent federal legislative authority"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Thirty-seventh Congress"}],"$L1c"]}],"$L1d","$L1e","$L1f"]]}]]}],"$L20"]]]}]
17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L21","/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep201-frederick-douglass.jpg","alt":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of Frederick Douglass on an oval studio mount.","caption":"Douglass insisted that the United States could not defeat slaveholders’ rebellion while protecting the labor system that made their war possible. This portrait dates to about 1862.","name":"Head-and-shoulders portrait of Frederick Douglass on an oval studio mount.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}],["$","$L21","/images/ep201-fort-monroe-contraband.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep201-fort-monroe-contraband.jpg","alt":"An 1861 racist caricature showing Black freedom seekers running toward Fort Monroe while a white enslaver chases them.","caption":"This 1861 printed card uses racist caricature and slurs while recording a contemporary understanding of Fort Monroe as a destination that enslaved people could reach and from which enslavers could not easily reclaim them.","name":"An 1861 racist caricature showing Black freedom seekers running toward Fort Monroe while a white enslaver chases them.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}]]}]]}]
